 "question": "Does any bore in this robot accept a heat-set insert?",
 "why_it_is_asked": "the community reconstruction's Purchase Estimate (reference/makerworld-3250889/upstream-github-fanhao375-microduck-replica/docs/fastener-reconstruction.en.md) suggests 60 M2 heat-set inserts, worded 'recommended over tapping printed plastic directly'. That is advice to a replica builder, not a reading of the product. The reference geometry is what we model from, so the reference geometry is what is asked.",
 "method": "a heat-set insert needs a straight bore of its own OUTER knurl diameter; a tap drill or a self-tap pilot is roughly half that. The diameter separates them with no judgement.",
 "counts": {
  "bores_measured": 355,
  "meshes": 38,
  "bores_in_an_insert_diameter_window": 37,
  "excluded_by_a_physical_test": 37,
  "surviving_insert_candidates": 0,
  "M2_M2.5_tap_drill_pilots": 46
 },
 "verdict": "NO HEAT-SET INSERT BORE EXISTS IN THIS GEOMETRY. Of 355 measured bores, 37 fall in an insert diameter window and every one of those 37 is excluded by a physical test, not by a preference: a counterbore is a screw-head seat over a clearance hole, and a bore shallower than 2.0 mm cannot hold any insert in the reference family. The 46 M2/M2.5 tap-drill pilots are what this design fastens into.",
 "settled_by": "a teardown photograph showing brass at the mouth of a boss, or a caliper on a real unit. Pollen's meshes carry no insert bore, and the meshes are what we modelled from \u2014 an insert in the real robot would be a finding about the reference, not about this search.",
